{
  "_readme": "GitHub owns the native method, event, tool, mode and permission names. The event list holds every type the installed runtime declares (read from copilot-sdk's generated session-event union), because the worker persists every non-ephemeral event and a type the browser cannot identify draws raw JSON. `eventPrefixes` covers the families where every member takes the same answer, so those members need no constant of their own. Its MEMBERSHIP crosses the boundary as well as its spellings, which is why it emits an ordered key slice: both sides must test every family, and a side that lists a subset by hand hides the rest of them behind a row nobody drew. The families are `model.` and `hook.`, which are the runtime's own trace, and `session.canvas.`, `factory.`, `assistant.fusion_` and `session.fusion_`, which are the experiments the IR plan holds out of scope. The tool list holds every name a transcript can hold, which is WIDER than what one release emits, because most of the names are conditional: a mode, a setting or an extension decides whether a session offers one. `session.tools.getCurrentMetadata` on 1.0.83 -- after `session.tools.initializeAndValidate`, which BUILDS the list a fresh session has not built yet -- answers 23 names, and `generic_tool_search`, `tool_search_tool` and `context_board` are not among them. Those three stay here, because a row an older release wrote still carries them and the reader must still recognize it. `permissionOutcomes` is how one permission ENDED. The runtime states it on `permission.completed` as `result.kind` -- `result` is an OBJECT and not a word, and each variant carries its own fields beside the kind, so a reader that took it for a string matched nothing. Five of the nine record an answer the reader gave, and the four `denied-by-*` ones record a refusal the runtime made on its own, with no request that ever reached a reader. `permissionDecisionSources` is the sibling `decisionSource` field, which says WHO decided. It is the ONLY field that separates an approval a PERSON gave from one an assisted-approval judge, a host policy, an unattended fallback or a replayed authorization record produced: all five return the same `result`, so a reader of `result` alone cannot tell a human grant from an automatic one. The runtime states it on `permission.completed`, where it is OPTIONAL -- absent on every completion a build before Copilot 1.0.84-5 wrote, and the SDK requires a consumer to read that absence as \"not a human decision\" rather than assuming one. It is also REQUIRED on `permission.carriedForward`, where it is always `authorization_carry_forward`, stated explicitly so a consumer cannot mistake a replayed grant for a new decision. Both the field and that event are marked experimental in the SDK and may change. LeapMux owns the session-mode option-group id and the approval-scope words its control surface sends.",
